---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import { sources, kindLabel, type SourceKind } from '../data/sources';
import { disputedSpecs, announcedSpecs } from '../data/specs';

const order: SourceKind[] = ['primary', 'independent', 'vendor', 'standard', 'method'];
const grouped = order.map((k) => ({ kind: k, items: sources.filter((s) => s.kind === k) }));
const unverified = sources.filter((s) => !s.verified);
---
<Base
  title="Sources & Method"
  description="What is measured, what is announced, what the sources disagree about, and which citations still need their deep link confirmed."
  kicker="Reference"
  heading="Sources & Method"
  slug="/sources"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      Three rules govern every number on this site: measured figures and announced figures are
      never mixed; where sources disagree the range is shown rather than a pick; and citations that
      have not been confirmed against the publisher are marked as such rather than quietly padding
      the list.
    </p>

    <h2 id="disputed">Figures the sources disagree about</h2>

    <p>
      These appear throughout the site marked <span style="color:var(--amber)">±</span>, and
      hovering one shows its range. Corroborate each against a primary datasheet before treating it
      as settled.
    </p>

    <div class="part-list bleed">
      {disputedSpecs.map((s) => (
        <div data-group="power">
          <h3>{s.label} <span class="count">{s.unit ? `${s.value} ${s.unit}` : s.value}</span></h3>
          <p><strong style="color:var(--amber)">{s.range}</strong></p>
          {s.note && <p>{s.note}</p>}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2 id="announced">Announced, not measured</h2>

    <p>
      Roadmap figures drawn from keynote material and supply-chain reporting. They are labelled at
      every point of use and are never placed beside benchmark results as if they were the same
      kind of claim.
    </p>

    <div class="part-list bleed">
      {announcedSpecs.map((s) => (
        <div data-group="cooling">
          <h3>{s.label} <span class="count">{s.unit ? `${s.value} ${s.unit}` : s.value}</span></h3>
          {s.note && <p>{s.note}</p>}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2 id="gaps">Known gaps</h2>

    <Callout kind="caveat" tag="Open item">
      <p>
        <strong>Rack power smoothing under synchronised training loads.</strong> Large training
        runs make a rack's draw behave like a square wave as thousands of GPUs enter and leave
        collectives together, and the mitigations involve capacitance or energy storage at rack or
        facility level. This is the thinnest-sourced topic in the research behind this site, and
        <a href="/power-cooling">Chapter 6</a> deliberately says little about it. Writing it up
        properly requires a dedicated NVIDIA or OCP power paper rather than inference from
        secondary commentary.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="caveat" tag="Open item">
      <p>
        <strong>{unverified.length} citations still need their deep link confirmed.</strong> They
        are marked "unconfirmed" in the list below. The claims they support come from the research
        behind this site, but the exact document has not been re-checked against the publisher —
        which should happen before this site is treated as a reference rather than an explainer.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="all">All sources</h2>

    {grouped.map(({ kind, items }) => items.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h3>{kindLabel[kind]}</h3>
        <div class="part-list bleed">
          {items.map((s) => (
            <div id={s.id} data-group={kind === 'primary' ? 'compute' : kind === 'independent' ? 'switch' : 'power'}>
              <h3>
                {s.url ? <a href={s.url} rel="noopener">{s.title}</a> : s.title}
                <span class="count">{s.publisher}</span>
                {!s.verified && <span class="count" style="color:var(--amber)">unconfirmed</span>}
              </h3>
              {s.note && <p>{s.note}</p>}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </>
    ))}

    <h2 id="method">Method</h2>

    <p>
      The fact base is a single module — every figure the site states is defined once, with its
      unit, its sources, any disputed range, and whether it is announced. Pages reference figures
      by identifier rather than typing numbers into prose, so a correction lands everywhere at once
      and it is not possible for two chapters to disagree with each other.
    </p>

    <p>
      The 3D rack is original geometry generated procedurally from those same dimensions. NVIDIA
      publishes no CAD for this rack, so an original model was required regardless; generating it
      from parameters rather than authoring a mesh keeps the dimensions next to the sourced figures
      they come from, ships no asset bytes, and leaves every part individually addressable for the
      exploded view. It is a schematic diagram in three dimensions, not a fabrication drawing.
    </p>

    <p>
      No NVIDIA artwork, branding or CAD is reproduced anywhere on this site. Word marks are used
      nominatively to describe the hardware. Mechanical proportions follow public teardown
      photography and openly licensed Open Compute Project documentation.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
